<!-- A deck changed by instruction (backend/ai.js).

     Rendered only where a key is configured. An instance without one does
     not get a disabled button, it gets no button -- the same way the PDF
     export is simply absent where no browser was found.

     A dialog and not a tab in the form, for the reason the bands dialog is
     one: this is about the whole DECK, the three tabs over there are about
     the slide in front. It is dragged aside and the preview is kept out of
     its veil (js/editor/dialog-aside.js), because the half minute this
     takes is a half minute one spends watching.

     What it holds is one field, and that is the whole of it: what should
     be different. The deck goes along by itself, and everything else a
     model would need to know -- the eleven layouts, every attribute, the
     limits, which pictures lie in this deck's folder -- is generated and
     sent without anybody being asked (backend/ai-format.js).

     Building a deck out of material is the other half of this and lives
     on the overview, where talks begin (partials/ai-new.ejs).

     Nothing in here writes. What comes back goes into the source dialog,
     coloured, cut into slides and checked, and is applied from there
     through the one door that saves (js/editor/deck-source.js). So the
     worst a prompt can do is waste a minute. -->
<dialog id="ai-dialog" class="dialog dialog-aside">
  <!-- The buttons sit in the head, where the source dialog's do: this
       dialog is scrolled through while it is filled in, and the one that
       starts the work must not scroll out of reach. Starting is a plain
       button and not a submit -- a submit would close the dialog, and the
       answer is what one came for. -->
  <form method="dialog" class="dialog-head">
    <h2><%= t('ai.changeTitle') %></h2>
    <span class="dialog-head-buttons">
      <button type="button" id="ai-start" class="button button-haupt"><%= t('ai.start') %></button>
      <button type="button" id="ai-stop" class="button" hidden><%= t('ai.cancel') %></button>
      <button id="ai-close" class="button button-quiet" value="close"><%= t('dialog.close') %></button>
    </span>
  </form>
  <div class="dialog-content">

    <!-- Who is actually writing, before the instruction is typed: the
         deck is changed by a model that is not part of trivialSlides, and
         it is sent there to be changed (ai.js, connection()). The same
         line stands over the dialog that BUILDS a deck
         (partials/ai-new.ejs). -->
    <p class="hint"><%= t('ai.whereChange', aiWhere) %></p>

    <p class="hint"><%= t('ai.changeHint') %></p>

    <div class="field">
      <label class="field-label" for="ai-prompt"><%= t('ai.prompt') %></label>
      <textarea id="ai-prompt" rows="4" maxlength="<%= aiPromptMax %>"
                placeholder="<%= t('ai.promptPlaceholderChange') %>"></textarea>
    </div>

    <!-- Which pictures the instruction may send for. The deck's library is
         where they come from, the model is told their names and picks from
         them, so it can never name a file that is not there
         (backend/ai-format.js). This is also the answer to the one limit
         of building on the overview: a deck made there has no pictures
         yet, and putting them on the slides is an instruction for here. -->
    <p id="ai-pictures" class="hint"></p>

    <!-- What it is writing, as it writes it. Not a spinner: this takes half
         a minute, and the one thing worth showing in it is the thing the
         user is about to have to judge. It is also where Cancel becomes
         meaningful -- a deck going visibly wrong can be stopped. -->
    <pre id="ai-stream" class="ai-stream" hidden aria-live="off"></pre>

    <p id="ai-state" class="ai-state" role="status"></p>

    <p class="hint prefs-scope"><%= t('ai.note') %></p>
  </div>
</dialog>
